    {
      "text": "Race modifies the positive association between socioeconomic status and BA degree completion: the SES gradient is significantly steeper for Black than White respondents and directionally steeper for Hispanic than White respondents.",
      "context": "Black, Hispanic, and White NLS participants with social-background and academic-resource measures at secondary school graduation; unweighted, associational estimates with 236 BA completers and sparse minority-subgroup events.",
      "variables": [
        "SES",
        "SAMPLE_RACE",
        "BA DEGREE COMPLETED"
      ],
      "relations": "SES is modeled linearly on the logit scale, standardized per one SD. BA-completion odds ratios are 2.07 (95% CI 1.47–2.90) for Black, 1.65 (1.11–2.44) for Hispanic, and 1.18 (1.02–1.36) for White respondents. The race×SES interaction is significant (2-df LR χ²=9.57, p=0.0083), robust to Firth penalization (p=0.0003), and driven primarily by the Black–White contrast. Bootstrap logit-slope differences are Black–White 0.55 (95% CI 0.17–0.91) and Hispanic–White 0.34 (−0.08–0.72); the latter does not establish a difference or equality."
    },
    {
      "text": "White respondents have higher absolute BA-completion rates than Black or Hispanic respondents across SES terciles, with the largest racial gap at low SES; SES alone does not explain the racial completion gap.",
      "context": "NLS participants grouped by race and low, middle, and high SES terciles; descriptive, noncausal comparisons.",
      "variables": [
        "SES",
        "SAMPLE_RACE",
        "BA DEGREE COMPLETED"
      ],
      "relations": "Low/middle/high SES-tercile completion rates are 3.05/3.54/3.81% for White, 0.44/1.87/2.22% for Black, and 0.78/1.73/3.08% for Hispanic respondents. Higher White absolute rates coexist with steeper minority SES gradients. Model-predicted completion for a low-SES White respondent (~2.9%) exceeds that for a high-SES Black respondent (~2.7%)."
    },
    {
      "text": "The race-dependent SES gradient in BA completion persists after adjustment for sex but attenuates to nonsignificance after additional adjustment for ability, class percentile, and family size.",
      "context": "Sensitivity analyses of the NLS race×SES logistic model; adjustment does not distinguish confounding from mediation or establish causality.",
      "variables": [
        "SES",
        "SAMPLE_RACE",
        "BA DEGREE COMPLETED",
        "SAMPLE_SEX",
        "ABILITY: COMPOSITE OF ASVAB SCORE",
        "PERCENTILE IN CLASS",
        "FAMILY SIZE OF SAMPLE"
      ],
      "relations": "With sex adjustment, the race×SES interaction remains significant (χ²=9.63, p=0.0081). Adding ability, class percentile, and family size attenuates the interaction (χ²=1.75, p=0.42), with SES×White OR shifting from 0.57 to 0.76 relative to Black respondents. Ability and class percentile are positively associated with completion (OR 2.33 and 1.83 per SD), while family size is negatively associated (OR 0.59 per person). Attenuation is consistent with academic-performance resources lying on the pathway, without resolving their mediator-versus-confounder status."
    }
